      "model": {
        "model_architecture": "yolov8",
        "description": "Baseline efficient detector suitable for CPU/Metal on Apple Silicon. Balances accuracy and model size, with broad tooling and community support for macOS/CoreML conversion and deployment.",
        "architecture_family": "yolo"
      }
    },
    "rationale": "The MacBook Air with Apple M4 has 16 GB unified memory and relies on CPU/Metal acceleration. Among the candidate architectures, yolov8 offers a favorable balance of detection quality and runtime efficiency on such hardware, with established workflows for CoreML/Metal deployment. It generally provides better accuracy than lightweight single-stage nets like SSD300 while remaining more CPU-friendly than heavier two-stage models (FasterRCNN/RetinaNet) and large RTDETR variants. Given the target mAP@0.5:0.95 around 0.30 and the need to run entirely locally, yolov8 is the most practical baseline that can reach the target with proper preprocessing and potential model size tuning (e.g., resolution, input scale) without requiring GPU acceleration. The other candidates are considered for fallback if yolov8 underperforms in practice or if deployment tooling limitations arise on macOS.",
